---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import GpaCalculator from '@/components/tools/everyday/GpaCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="gpa-calculator">
  <GpaCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to calculate your GPA</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        List this semester's courses with their credit hours and the letter grade you earned or expect.
      </li>
      <li>
        Set honours, AP or IB courses in the level dropdown so the weighted GPA adds the bonus.
      </li>
      <li>
        Add your GPA and total credits from before this semester to get the new cumulative GPA.
      </li>
      <li>
        Use the target solver to find the average you need in your remaining credits to reach a goal GPA.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The GPA formula</h2>
    <p><strong>GPA = Σ(credits × grade points) ÷ Σ(credits)</strong></p>
    <p>
      Credits × grade points are quality points. A 4-credit course with a B (3.0) earns 12 quality points.
      A 3-credit A− (3.7) earns 11.1. Add the quality points, add the credits, and divide:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Course</th>
            <th scope="col">Credits</th>
            <th scope="col">Grade</th>
            <th scope="col">Points</th>
            <th scope="col">Quality points</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>English Literature</td><td>3</td><td>A−</td><td>3.7</td><td>11.1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Calculus</td><td>4</td><td>B+</td><td>3.3</td><td>13.2</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Chemistry</td><td>4</td><td>B</td><td>3.0</td><td>12.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>History</td><td>3</td><td>A</td><td>4.0</td><td>12.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>14</strong></td><td></td><td></td><td><strong>48.3</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>48.3 ÷ 14 = <strong>3.45 GPA</strong>.</p>
    <p>
      Credits drive the result, and the number of courses does not. A 4-credit course pulls the average
      twice as hard as a 2-credit one, so dropping a heavy course changes a GPA much more than dropping a
      light one.
    </p>

    <h2>Weighted versus unweighted GPA</h2>
    <p>
      An unweighted GPA caps each grade at 4.0 for all course levels. A weighted GPA adds a bonus for
      harder classes, usually +0.5 for honours and +1.0 for AP or IB, so a weighted average can exceed 4.0.
      A straight-A student in all AP courses has 5.0.
    </p>
    <p>
      High schools use the weighted GPA for class rank and honour rolls. University admissions offices
      usually calculate their own unweighted GPA from your transcript, with their own course list and
      scale, so the unweighted number is the more portable one.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Letter</th>
            <th scope="col">Regular</th>
            <th scope="col">Honours (+0.5)</th>
            <th scope="col">AP / IB (+1.0)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>A</td><td>4.0</td><td>4.5</td><td>5.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A−</td><td>3.7</td><td>4.2</td><td>4.7</td></tr>
          <tr><td>B+</td><td>3.3</td><td>3.8</td><td>4.3</td></tr>
          <tr><td>B</td><td>3.0</td><td>3.5</td><td>4.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C</td><td>2.0</td><td>2.5</td><td>3.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>D</td><td>1.0</td><td>1.5</td><td>2.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>F</td><td>0.0</td><td>0.0</td><td>0.0</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Cumulative GPA</h2>
    <p>
      Your cumulative GPA is the average of all courses you have taken, weighted by credits. It is not the
      average of your semester GPAs. First get your banked quality points:
    </p>
    <p><strong>banked points = old GPA × old credits</strong></p>
    <p>
      <strong>new cumulative = (banked points + this semester's points) ÷ (old credits + new credits)</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      A 3.42 across 45 credits carries 153.9 quality points. Add the 48.3 points and 14 credits above
      and the cumulative becomes 202.2 ÷ 59 = <strong>3.43</strong>. The more credits you have banked,
      the less one semester can move the number. So a low first-year GPA is hard to recover from, and a
      strong one is hard to lose.
    </p>

    <h2>Solving for a target GPA</h2>
    <p><strong>needed = (target × (banked credits + future credits) − banked points) ÷ future credits</strong></p>
    <p>
      To lift a 3.43 across 59 credits up to a 3.60 with 15 credits left: (3.60 × 74 − 202.2) ÷ 15 =
      (266.4 − 202.2) ÷ 15 = <strong>4.28</strong>. That is above 4.0, so it is not reachable in one
      semester, and the solver tells you so. Over 30 credits, the same goal needs 3.93.
    </p>

    <h2>Details that trip people up</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Pass/fail courses usually carry credit but no grade points, so leave them out of the list. If you
        enter them as an A, the result is too high.
      </li>
      <li>
        A repeated course may replace the original grade or be averaged with it, by the institution's
        policy. Enter the grade your registrar counts.
      </li>
      <li>
        Transfer credits often count towards the degree but not towards the GPA.
      </li>
      <li>
        An A+ is 4.0 at many institutions and 4.3 at others. Use the switch to match yours.
      </li>
      <li>
        Withdrawals usually carry no credits and no points, so they are not in the calculation.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Your courses, grades and transcript figures stay in this browser tab and are not uploaded.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
